{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>For this time I am sending all my plagues.</b> Rashi explained: we learn that the plague of the firstborn is equal to all the plagues. The Ra'am explained: בְּכוּרוֹת הַבֵּי״ת — the letter bet of בְּכוֹרוֹת is pointed with a patach, because in the hail they were stricken only with regard to the produce that had ripened early, as it says, ״כִּי הַשְּׂעֹרָה אָבִיב״ (Exodus 9:31) — Rashi explained בִּכְּרָה כְּבָר, it had already ripened. And there are those who have the reading ״בַּצּוֹרוֹת״ in place of ״בְּכוֹרוֹת״, for the hail caused them famine and drought, and so Mahari'a read. Nevertheless, the questioner's line of reasoning is not silenced: why was this plague greater than all the plagues? And one cannot say that it was great because of the great terror that came from ״קוֹל ה׳ חֹצֵב לַהֲבוֹת אֵשׁ״ (Psalms 29:7), for those are truly terrifying things — but if so, why did Rashi hang the matter on the fact that the first-ripened produce or the drought was stricken, when surely the sounds are the essence of the terror? And on the contrary, it appears that they were not so particular about the grain, for if they were, then the plague of locusts would be equal to all the plagues, since after all it ate what the hail had left. Rather, it is certain that they were not so particular, since the wheat and the spelt were not stricken. If so, why should this plague be greater than all the plagues?" "Now according to the plain sense we can settle this, that it speaks literally of the plague of the firstborn. For one must examine what is meant by what is said: ״כִּי בַּפַּעַם הַזֹּאת אֲנִי שֹׁלֵחַ כָּל מַגֵּפֹתַי אֶל לִבְּךָ״ — why is the expression ״אֶל לִבְּךָ״ not said for any plague except this one? Rather, it means to say: in the order of the plagues, I am sending you one plague that will be such that if you are wise, you will take to heart the plague of the firstborn that will come at the end of the order of the plagues — for from the very beginning HaShem warned Pharaoh about the plague of the firstborn, as it is written (Exodus 4:23) ״וַתְּמָאֵן לְשַׁלְּחוֹ הִנֵּה אָנֹכִי הֹרֵג אֶת בִּנְךָ בְּכֹרֶךָ״, and as Rashi explained there. And since at that time he did not yet feel how great this evil was, because he did not see it but only heard the sound of words, therefore he did not take it to heart and would not turn back because of a mere sound. Therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, said: now I am sending you a plague upon the first fruits of the earth's produce, and I know that in this plague you will have far greater distress than in all the previous plagues, because every fruit that ripens early is more beloved to a person, as will be explained with HaShem's help in parashat Ki Tavo (Deuteronomy 26:2) regarding the reason for first fruits. And the proof is that in this plague he confessed and said ״חָטָאתִי הַפָּעַם״ — what he did not say in all the previous plagues, because in truth he was greatly pained that the fruits that had ripened early were stricken, since they are more beloved to a person. And if regarding the fruit of the earth's produce it is so, how much the more so regarding the fruit of your womb — how great the distress at the death of your son, your firstborn! And if until now you have not felt the matter, behold, this time, when I strike the first fruits of the earth's produce, then you will take to heart all my plagues — that is, the plague of the firstborn of the fruit of the womb — how great the distress is. And concerning this it is said: ״כִּי בַּפַּעַם הַזֹּאת אֲנִי שֹׁלֵחַ אֶת כָּל מַגֵּפוֹתַי״ — that is, the plague of the firstborn of the fruit of the womb — ״אֶל לִבֶּךָ״, that you should set your heart to contemplate and to derive a kal va-chomer: if regarding the first fruits of the earth's produce the distress is so great, since they are beloved to a person, how much the more so regarding the first fruits of the fruit of your womb! And if so, Rashi spoke well in saying that the plague of the firstborn itself is equal to all the plagues, for after all Scripture called it ״כָּל מַגֵּפוֹתַי״. And so it is the truth, for in all of them Pharaoh was not humbled except in the plague of the firstborn. And through this is settled why Scripture needed to say ״וְהַפִּשְׁתָּה וְהַשְּׂעֹרָה נֻכָּתָה כִּי הַשְּׂעֹרָה אָבִיב״ and it had already ripened — and this is a very precious interpretation." "And by way of allusion, it appears to me along the lines of what I wrote above (7:17), that the plague of hail and the sounds came upon Pharaoh because he did not wish to hear the voice of YHWH in glory, and therefore HaShem made terrible and terrifying sounds reach his ear, and therefore he confessed specifically regarding this sin and said ״ה׳ הַצַּדִּיק וַאֲנִי וְעַמִּי הָרְשָׁעִים״ (Exodus 9:27). In place of his having denied YHWH and said, 'It is not He,' and his having sinned with sound, and reported evil speech about his Creator, therefore he was punished with sounds, in the manner that they said (Leviticus Rabbah 16:7) regarding the metzora, that he brings birds, which make a sound, to atone for the sound of evil speech. And therefore it says here ״הַקֹּלוֹת יֶחְדָּלוּן וְהַבָּרָד לֹא יִהְיֶה עוֹד״ (Exodus 9:29). And it is difficult: why join them and say ״הַקֹּלוֹת וְהַבָּרָד יֶחְדָּלוּן״ or ״לֹא יִהְיֶה עוֹד״? Rather, this is what Moses said to Pharaoh: Why do you cry out to me, saying, ״הַעְתִּירוּ אֶל ה׳״? If you turn back from your way and do not send forth outside your voice — a voice of outcry with revilings and blasphemies toward Above, saying ״מִי ה׳״ — then automatically the hail will be no more, for when the cause is removed, the effect is removed as well. And this is what it said: When your sounds cease, then automatically the hail will be no more, לְמַעַן תֵּדַע כִּי לַה׳ הָאָרֶץ (Exodus 9:29), when you return in repentance — not as you said at the outset, ״מִי ה׳״ (Exodus 5:2)." "And because this sin was in the tongue, and it is known that the sin of the tongue magnifies iniquities against the three principal transgressions — and they are idolatry, forbidden sexual relations, and bloodshed — and the whole Torah is included, for whoever acknowledges idolatry is as one who denies the whole Torah in its entirety; if so, it is fitting that one who sins with the tongue should be punished with all the modes of death, for the punishment of idolatry is stoning, and likewise the punishment of the blasphemer, and the punishment of bloodshed is slaying, and the punishment of unspecified forbidden sexual relations is strangulation, and a priest's daughter who commits harlotry is burned. If so, we find that all four modes of court-imposed death are applicable to these three principal transgressions, and therefore it is fitting that one who sins with the tongue should be punished with all these plagues, for it is equivalent to all of them. And this is what it said: ״כִּי בַּפַּעַם הַזֹּאת אֲנִי שֹׁלֵחַ אֶת כָּל מַגֵּפֹתַי״, for we find in the plague of hail a paradigm for all four court-imposed deaths: stoning with stones — a paradigm for this is that hail and firestones descended upon them, and it would pelt them. Burning, as it says: ״וַתִּהֲלַךְ אֵשׁ אָרְצָה״. Slaying, as it says (Psalms 78:47) ״יַהֲרֹג בַּבָּרָד גַּפְנָם״, and the expression of slaying does not apply to a vine, but it alluded to their precious men who are likened to a vine, as is found there (Psalms 80:9) ״גֶּפֶן מִמִּצְרַיִם תַּסִּיעַ״. And strangulation — that is, through the abundance of water that came down with the hail, for perhaps some of them drowned in the field in the downpour of hail, for whoever is liable to strangulation drowns in a river, etc. (Ketubot 30a)." "And the hail struck that which had ripened first, in place of Pharaoh having sent the hand of his tongue against the Holy One, blessed be He, against the firstborn of the world, as is found in Sefer Neveh Shalom. And incidentally, Scripture informed us that the flax and the barley were stricken, etc. — even though according to its plain sense it seems to come to inform us incidentally that just as the flax was stricken because it stood rigid in its stalks, so was Pharaoh stricken for the hardness of his neck like this stalk. Nevertheless, incidentally, along our path there also comes the matter of informing who and who are the stricken and afflicted ones who suffer damage from the masters of the tongue, and who is free from the masters of the tongue — so that it should all be measure for measure: everyone who suffers damage from the outcry of the masters of the tongue, correspondingly those plants that resemble it were also stricken by the sound of the hail. And everyone who escapes the sound of the masters of the tongue, those plants that resemble it also escaped the sound of the hail.\n\nFor it is well known that from the masters of the tongue two classes suffer damage: one class is the firstborn in eminence, against whom many zealots attack, who are jealous of the greatness of their stature. And furthermore, because of the greatness of their stature, they are more deeply affected by evil speech — unlike one who is among those of lesser stature, with whom people are not so jealous and who do not direct the arrow of their tongue at them so much. And if you say that his arrows did land upon him, nevertheless he is not so deeply affected and it does not harm him as much as the tongue harms men of eminence who hold the right of the firstborn, who sit first in the kingdom of heaven. Is not this quality prevalent among many of our people's sons, for the masters of the tongue made it heard that there is none clean, every man of valor many times over, and with their tongue they topple every high wall and every mighty cedar in the Lebanon they bore through — the grub of Jacob.\n\nThe second class is those who stand against the masters of the tongue like this stalk, and do not yield to be among those who hear their shame and do not respond, but rather wishes to stand against them to answer his reviler, or one who prides himself in his own eminence, for this is his stature, likened to a palm tree — upon such a one does evil speech harm the most.\n\n[Translator's note: In the original Hebrew text, the final passage discussing the two classes of those harmed by evil speech appears to continue beyond what is typically a comment's natural conclusion, blending into a social critique of his generation. The references to 'אין נקי' (none clean), 'כל איש חיל רב פעלים' (every man of valor, many deeds), and 'תולעת יעקב' (the grub of Jacob) appear to be allusions to contemporary figures or conditions that remain textually dense and possibly localized references.]" "And one who escapes evil speech is one of those who darken themselves, who do not show themselves among people, to fulfill what is stated (Isaiah 26:20): ״לֵךְ עַמִּי בֹּא בַחֲדָרֶיךָ וּסְגֹר דְּלָתְךָ בַּעֲדֶךָ חֲבִי כִמְעַט רֶגַע עַד יַעֲבָר זָעַם״. Even though ״חֵץ שָׁחוּט לְשׁוֹנָם״ (Jeremiah 9:7) — 'their tongue is a sharpened arrow' — passes even through several walls, and one who sits in chambers within chambers and is saved from every weapon, is nevertheless not saved from these masters of arrows. In any event, there is at least some deliverance when a person does not display himself before them with his virtues and does not answer his reviler anything, and he will be ״כְּאִישׁ אֲשֶׁר אֵינֶנּוּ שֹׁמֵעַ״ (Psalms 38:14). Therefore the Torah told us that the flax and the barley were struck, ״כִּי הַשְּׂעֹרָה אָבִיב וְהַפִּשְׁתָּה גִּבְעֹל״ (Exodus 9:31). And the question is: what does this come to teach us? For if it comes to teach that because the wheat and the spelt were not struck, therefore Pharaoh hardened his heart — in any case, why was it necessary to give a reason why these were struck and those were not struck? Rather, it hints to us: just as the voice of evil speech harms the foremost ones and strikes one who stands against them like this stalk, so were the flax and the barley struck by the voice of the hail — for the flax was in stalk, standing in its full height, and the barley was in the ear, already fully grown. But the wheat and the spelt were not struck. So too all who darken themselves and are not seen by people in their virtue, and make themselves as though they do not hear — these were not struck. And this will be explained, with the help of YHWH, in the parashat Ki Teitse (Deuteronomy 23:14) on the verse ״וְיָתֵד תִּהְיֶה לְךָ עַל אֲזֵנֶךָ״, for silence is the primary weapon of a person, with which he defeats his revilers." "And with this one may resolve what is written: ״כְּצֵאתִי אֶת הָעִיר אֶפְרֹשׂ אֶת כַּפַּי אֶל ה׳״ (Exodus 9:29). Rashi explained: because the city was full of idols. And the questioners ask here: why was he insistent specifically in this plague? And although according to its plain sense one may say that surely in all the plagues he was insistent on this, and Moses had no need to inform Pharaoh when he would pray and whether inside the city or outside it, except in this plague, where Pharaoh said ״וְרַב מִהְיוֹת קֹלוֹת אֱלֹהִים״ (Exodus 9:28) — hear from this that he was so panicked by the voices that he wanted them to cease immediately. Therefore Moses needed to inform him that it was impossible for them to cease immediately, for he needed to go out of the city first, and there he would entreat YHWH. Nevertheless, according to our approach as well, he hinted to him that the sin of the tongue caused Pharaoh to have YHWH depart from him, and He does not wish to be seen even by Moses as long as he is in one chamber together with Pharaoh. Along the lines of what our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Arakhin 15b): 'Everyone who speaks evil speech — the Holy One, blessed be He, said: I and he cannot dwell together, as it is stated (Psalms 101:5): מְלָשְׁנִי בַסֵּתֶר רֵעֵהוּ אוֹתוֹ אַצְמִית, גְּבַהּ עֵינַיִם וּרְחַב לֵבָב אֹתוֹ לֹא אוּכָל — do not read אוֹתוֹ ('him') but אִתּוֹ ('with him').' Therefore it is stated ״כְּצֵאתִי אֶת הָעִיר אֶפְרֹשׂ כַּפַּי אֶל ה׳״, because the city was full of idols. For just as He sent upon him all His plagues because evil speech multiplies sins against idolatry and forbidden sexual relations and bloodshed, so He referred to a tongue 'full of idols' to teach that this sin encompasses many idols and fills the entire city with all kinds of idols." "And some say that he needed to pray in the field, the place where the plague was most active. And it appears one may say further, that he needed to say ״כְּצֵאתִי אֶת הָעִיר״, so that Pharaoh would not suspect him that out of fear of the voices and the hail he would go into some house to pray. Therefore he said to him ״כְּצֵאתִי אֶת הָעִיר״, to show him the greatness of the miracle: that in every place upon which the sole of his foot would tread, there would be no hail."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {0 "The reading attributed to 'הרא״ם' and 'מהרי״א' — I have transcribed these as given in the source text without expanding the abbreviations, as the referents are uncertain.", 1 "In the first comment, 'הרא״ם' is likely Rabbi Eliyahu Mizrahi; 'מהרי״א' is likely Mahar'i (Rabbi Joseph) — left as transliterations per source.", 3 "In comment 3, the citation '(כתובות ל:)' is rendered as '(Ketubot 30a)' following the standard Talmudic reference format.", 4 "The final section of comment 4 appears to contain an extended social critique with possible allusions to contemporary conditions; the translation preserves the dense, allusive quality of the original Hebrew without expanding the references.", 6 "The closing phrase reads literally: 'so He mentioned a tongue [as] full of idols,' apparently equating Pharaoh's evil speech with idolatry itself through a play on Rashi's rationale."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:19:49.606076147Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}